{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Now the flax and the barley, etc.</b> Scripture reported what happened, and I do not know why these two verses were inserted in this place, before Moses finished his prayer and the hail ceased. And they said in the name of the Gaon Rav Saadiah that they are the words of Moses to Pharaoh — that he said to him, before the warning, 'that you may know that there is none like HaShem our Elohim' (Exodus 9:14): the flax and the barley have already been struck, and this cannot be remedied, but the wheat and the emmer were not yet struck, and from now on nothing will be ruined for you.\n\nAnd I find no satisfactory reasoning in this interpretation, for the hail struck all the vegetation of the field and broke every tree, and the wheat and the emmer were spared only because they are late-growing — they had not sprouted at all, or because of their smallness they were not ruined since they will yet grow. If so, even if hail fell upon them again for many days it would not harm them, and there is no need to inform him of what was ruined and what was not ruined, for when the hail ceases he will see. And in my opinion these are the words of Moses to Pharaoh, who said to them: I know that before the plagues cease you will fear, and afterward you will relapse in your foolishness; but <b>the flax and the barley were struck, and the wheat and the emmer</b> — which are for all your livestock — <b>were not struck</b> in this plague, and they are in the hand of Elohim to destroy them from you if you again turn and sin before Him. He hinted to them concerning what He said (Exodus 10:5), 'and it shall eat the residue of that which has remained to you from the hail.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:05:11.098749716Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}